[{:seon.task/name "a-method-reads-the-fields-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 by the worktree session datom-cost, profiling Datahike's add path (task datahike.transaction/a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts). Jolt's defrecord bound every declared field on a method's entry with (get this :f), ~6 ticks a field in the runtime spine whatever the body read (deftype read each by slot, ~2 ticks a field). A protocol call on Datahike's 21-field DB cost 158 ticks against 37 on a 2-field record, and the add path makes ~30 such calls per datom (dbi/-config 18, dbi/-attrs-by 11). Clojure's own methods see every field, so the macros keep binding all of them, defrecord through clojure.core/__record-get (get under its own name: --opt lowers it to the shape's accessor as it did get). The decision is made on the analyzed body: Jolt's fold pass (its drop-unread-fields, in const-fold, every mode's pass) drops a __record-get or __deftype-slot bind on the method's own instance that nothing later reads; a get on any other value is never dropped (a sorted map's comparator or a type's valAt may throw or act). A field reached through a macro, named like a clojure.core var, or read in a closure or a later binding is a :local of the expanded body and stays bound: Jolt's unit suite field-binds. clojure.core/update gains Clojure's fixed arities: its variadic one alone consed a rest list per call (656 B on the DB record where the assoc is 208). Jolt jolt/record-field-binds 0d39edc3 (its first commit 3a3e35e6 scanned the written body, which misses a macro's field; superseded on the branch). A protocol call on a record is ~20-25 ticks whatever its width."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.record-cost-test/a-protocol-call-costs-what-its-method-reads}}]
